<Aside>
<strong>Image vs. support.</strong> The <em>image</em> $X(\Omega)=\&#123;X(\omega):\omega\in\Omega\&#125;$
is just the range of $X$ as a function: every value $X$ can in principle produce. The
<em>support</em> of $\mathbb&#123;P&#125;_X$ is the smaller object: the set of values where probability
actually lives. For $X\sim\mathrm&#123;Uniform&#125;[0,1]$ the image is $[0,1]$, but
$\mathbb&#123;P&#125;_X(\&#123;x\&#125;)=0$ for every single $x$. The support is the interval, not the
individual points. Image is a set-theoretic fact about the function; support is a
probabilistic fact about the pushforward measure.
</Aside>

<p>
The "distribution of $X$" is not really a thing that lives on $\Omega$; it lives on
$\mathbb&#123;R&#125;$. It is the <strong>pushforward measure</strong>
</p>

$$ \mathbb&#123;P&#125;_X(B) \;=\; \mathbb&#123;P&#125;\bigl(X^&#123;-1&#125;(B)\bigr), \qquad B\subseteq\mathbb&#123;R&#125;. $$

<p>
That is, the probability that $X$ lands in $B$ is the $\mathbb&#123;P&#125;$-measure of the set
of outcomes that $X$ sends into $B$. Pushing a measure forward by a function is the
abstract version of "change of variables."
</p>

<p>
In Figure 3, $\Omega=[0,1]$ carries Lebesgue measure (uniform). Drag the function
$X$, and watch the induced distribution on the $y$-axis appear as a histogram. The
teal band is a measurable set $B$ in the target space; its highlighted preimage
$X^&#123;-1&#125;(B)$ is the event in $\Omega$ whose measure defines $\mathbb&#123;P&#125;_X(B)$. A steep
piece of $X$ stretches a small interval in $\Omega$ over a wide range in $\mathbb&#123;R&#125;$,
so the pushforward density there is small; a flat piece concentrates mass.
</p>

