<Aside>
<strong>Why bother?</strong> Two reasons. First, on uncountable spaces you cannot
just "assign probability to each outcome": almost every singleton has measure zero,
so densities are the only way to talk about likelihood. Second, treating $X$ as a
function lets us cleanly compose: $g(X)$ is just another random variable, and its
distribution is the pushforward of $\mathbb&#123;P&#125;_X$ by $g$.
</Aside>

<p>
The pushforward is <em>functorial</em>: a single measurable map $X\colon\Omega\to E$
acts simultaneously on three layers. Sample spaces transform covariantly
($\omega\mapsto X(\omega)$), $\sigma$-algebras transform <em>contra</em>variantly
(events on the target pull back to events on the source via $X^&#123;-1&#125;$), and
measures transform covariantly again (each $\mu$ on $\Omega$ produces $X_*\mu$
on $E$). The diagram below shows all three flavors of the same map.
</p>

<Figure title={String.raw`Figure 3c · Pushforward as a functor: $X$ acting on spaces, $\sigma$-algebras, and measures`} canvasId="fig-pushforward-cat" width="760" height="320">
