<h2>3. Absolute continuity between two measures</h2>

<p>
Now suppose we have <em>two</em> measures on the same measurable space
$(\Omega,\mathcal&#123;F&#125;)$; call them <span class="mtag mu">$\mu$</span> and
<span class="mtag nu">$\nu$</span>. We say $\nu$ is
<strong>absolutely continuous</strong> with respect to $\mu$, written
$\nu\ll\mu$, if every $\mu$-null set is also $\nu$-null:
</p>

$$ \mu(A)=0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \nu(A)=0. $$

<p>
Intuitively, $\nu$ cannot put mass where the reference measure $\mu$ refuses to look.
If $\mu$ is
Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb&#123;R&#125;$ and $\nu$ has a point mass at $x_0$, then
$\nu\not\ll\mu$ because $\&#123;x_0\&#125;$ has $\mu$-measure zero but $\nu$-measure positive.
</p>

<p>
Drag the two distributions below. The shaded regions show where each measure puts
mass. The red badge lights up when $\nu\ll\mu$ fails: when $\nu$ has positive
mass in a region where $\mu$ has none.
</p>

