<h2>Where this goes next</h2>

<p>
The same measure-change identity appears in several places:
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Statistics.</strong> The likelihood ratio in a Neyman–Pearson test is a Radon–Nikodym derivative of one hypothesis's law with respect to another.</li>
  <li><strong>Stochastic calculus.</strong> Girsanov's theorem says that under mild conditions you can change the drift of a Brownian motion by multiplying by an explicit exponential martingale, and that martingale is exactly $dQ/dP$.</li>
  <li><strong>Machine learning.</strong> The KL divergence is $\mathbb&#123;E&#125;_\nu[\log d\nu/d\mu]$. Importance sampling, off-policy correction, and most "reweighting" tricks are change-of-measure in disguise.</li>
  <li><strong>Information theory.</strong> Mutual information is a KL divergence between the joint and the product of marginals, a Radon–Nikodym derivative on a product space.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Once you stop thinking of a density as <em>the</em> probability and start thinking of
it as <em>a relationship between two measures</em>, several formulas collapse to one
statement:
$\int g\, d\nu = \int g \cdot \tfrac&#123;d\nu&#125;&#123;d\mu&#125;\, d\mu$.
</p>

